0 g
BT /F2 14.5 Tf 58.00 678.75 Td (L4 Load Balancer) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 652.90 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 652.90 Td (An L4 load balancer operates at the transport layer, and so it does not have access to URLs, HTTP) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 639.50 Td (payloads, headers, etc. Instead, it just utilizes the IP and port information to establish and route the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 626.10 Td (connection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 610.20 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 610.20 Td (To forward the request to a backend server, it typically performs NAT at the load balancer, where the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 596.80 Td (destination is rewritten from the load balancer to the backend server.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 580.90 Td (So the TCP connection from the client is forwarded to the server through the load balancer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 561.00 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 561.00 Td (The downside is that since it cannot see the application payload or HTTP headers, things like caching,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 547.60 Td (header modifications, or content-based routing are not possible.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 531.70 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 531.70 Td (Because it simply forwards TCP packets without inspecting the payload in a single TCP connection, so it is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 518.30 Td (fast.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 502.40 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 502.40 Td (If traffic is raw TCP/UDP \(like for databases\) -> pick L4.) Tj ET
BT /F2 14.5 Tf 58.00 470.50 Td (L7 Load Balancer) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 444.65 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 444.65 Td (An L7 load balancer operates at the application layer, and so it can see URLs, HTTP headers, cookies,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 431.25 Td (methods, and sometimes even parts of the payload. It uses this application-level data to route requests, not) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 417.85 Td (just IP and port.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 401.95 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 401.95 Td (To forward traffic to backend services, it typically terminates the client tcp connection at the load balancer) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 388.55 Td (and then creates a separate tcp connection from the load balancer to the backend server.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 372.65 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 372.65 Td (an L7 load balancer also terminates TLS \(HTTPS\) meaning the client establishes an encrypted TLS) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 359.25 Td (connection to the LB, the LB decrypts the request so it can inspect URLs/headers for routing, and then it) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 345.85 Td (forwards the request to the backend \(either re-encrypted or plain HTTP\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 329.95 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 329.95 Td (The upside is that since it can inspect HTTP, it enables things like path-based routing , header rewrites,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 316.55 Td (sticky sessions, caching, rate limiting etc.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 300.65 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 300.65 Td (The downside is that because it has to terminate connections \(2 tcp connectins being done\) and parse) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 287.25 Td (HTTP and decrypt TLS, it adds more processing overhead than L4, so it\222s typically slower than a pure L4) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 273.85 Td (forwarder.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 257.95 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 257.95 Td (If traffic is http \(like for REST APIs \) -> pick L7) Tj ET
