BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 778.00 Td (Answer:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 758.10 Td (If consumers were allowed to read partitions independently without any coordination, then every consumer could) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 744.70 Td (end up reading the same partitions and hence the same messages, leading to duplicate processing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 724.80 Td (Kafka solves this by introducing consumer groups. A consumer group is just a logical grouping of consumers) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 711.40 Td (identified by the same group.id. When multiple consumers join the same group, Kafka treats them like a team) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 698.00 Td (that wants to share the work, and it enforces a very important rule:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 678.10 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 678.10 Td (Each partition is assigned to at most one consumer within the group) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 662.20 Td (A consumer can read from one or more partitions, but no two consumers in the same group will read from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 648.80 Td (same partition at the same time. This keeps ordering intact \(since each partition is an ordered log\) and ensures) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 635.40 Td (that each message is processed only once per consumer group.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 615.50 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 615.50 Td (If number of consumers in a consumer group < number of partitions of the topic it is consuming from, each) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 602.10 Td (consumer is assigned multiple partitions) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 586.20 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 586.20 Td (If number of consumers in a consumer group == number of partitions of the topic it is consuming from, each) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 572.80 Td (consumer is assigned exactly one partition) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 556.90 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 556.90 Td (If number of consumers in a consumer group > number of partitions of the topic it is consuming from, extra) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 543.50 Td (consumers remain idle for that topic) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 527.60 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 527.60 Td (If a consumer group subscribes to multiple topics, this assignment logic is applied independently per topic) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 511.70 Td (Now remember the offset we briefly introduced earlier as the position number \(0, 1, 2, and so on\) of a message) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 498.30 Td (inside a partition. This is exactly why offsets are useful. As consumers read from a partition, Kafka tracks how far) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 484.90 Td (they have read using offsets, and these offsets are tracked per consumer group, per partition.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 465.00 Td (Kafka stores these committed offsets inside an internal Kafka topic called __consumer_offsets, which is how) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 451.60 Td (Kafka can remember progress even if a consumer crashes or restarts. Because of this, consumers can resume) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 438.20 Td (from where they left off after failures, and different consumer groups can consume the same topic independently) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 424.80 Td (with their own progress.) Tj ET
