BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 694.75 Td (Messaging systems are not made for large payloads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 674.85 Td (When there is a large payload to send across two services, it can be pushed through a messaging system only) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 661.45 Td (up to its message size limits.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 641.55 Td (Those limits can be increased, but only up to what the messaging system can realistically support.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 621.65 Td (We can go with chunking like splitting the payload into smaller messages. It works, but it introduces batching) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 608.25 Td (logic, complexity of combining chunks together etc.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 588.35 Td (A cleaner solution would be to use the Claim-Check pattern:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 568.45 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 568.45 Td (Store the large payload in external storage) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 552.55 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 552.55 Td (Publish a small message containing a reference \(object key\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 536.65 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 536.65 Td (Let consumers fetch the payload only when needed through the token) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 520.75 Td (So now the messaging system carries just metadata and not megabytes of the payloads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 500.85 Td (One important detail is that this becomes a two-step operation:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 480.95 Td (\225) Tj ET
